THOUGHT: Now I understand the vulnerability. The `selReadStream` function has a heap buffer overflow when `sscanf` tries to write up to 200 bytes into a `selname` string that was allocated with `stringNew(linebuf)` which only allocates enough for the line buffer content. But the issue is that `selname` was a heap-allocated string of size matching `linebuf`, and `sscanf` with `%200s` can write up to 200 characters plus null terminator into it.

But wait - the fix changes `selname` from a heap-allocated pointer to a stack buffer of 256 bytes. The original code used `stringNew(linebuf)` which allocates `strlen(linebuf)+1` bytes. If `linebuf` is short but the name after "------" is long, `sscanf` would overflow.

